<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Auguste Blanqui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1805–1881</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Auguste Blanqui was a French revolutionary socialist who spent more than thirty years of his life in prison. He held that a small, disciplined group of conspirators should seize power and set up a temporary dictatorship on behalf of the people, a doctrine known as Blanquism. Arrested just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Commune of 1871, he was elected to its council in his absence, and his followers formed one of its main factions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-auguste-blanqui.jpg" alt="Louis Auguste Blanqui" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Appert, Eugène (Châteauroux, 13–03–1830), photographe · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blanqui_Louis-Auguste_(1805-1881)._R%C3%A9volutionnaire_et_activiste,_il_est_de_toutes_les_manifestations_qui_pr%C3%B4ne_l%27_%C3%A9gal,_PH4142-49_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
